# User Journeys and Workflows

The typical customer journey begins with browsing the catalog and adding items to a cart. This is followed by checkout, where the system validates stock and calculates the total. After successful payment processing, the order is confirmed.

Post-order confirmation, the warehouse team picks, packs, and ships the items, which automatically decrements inventory. Finally, the customer receives a shipping confirmation including tracking information.

A key success metric for the project is achieving 95% of pilot merchants reporting faster order processing, indicating improved efficiency in workflows.

> source: examples/sources/BUSINESS_DOMAIN_KNOWLEDGE.md | weight: 1.0 | hash: ae5c880e

Customers are expected to be able to browse and search the product catalog, add items to their cart, place orders, view their order history, and track shipments. This covers the full lifecycle of a customer interaction with the system.

Administrators need the capability to add, update, and remove products from the catalog. Additionally, they must be able to view and manage all orders placed by customers, providing comprehensive control over the product and order lifecycle.

Customers must be able to view a paginated list of products. The default pagination setting is 20 products per page, ensuring a manageable browsing experience.

Upon placing an order, customers must receive a confirmation within 3 seconds. This sets a strict performance requirement for a critical user interaction.

Admins must be able to perform Create, Read, Update, and Delete (CRUD) operations on products. These operations will be facilitated through a dedicated administrative API.

> source: examples/sources/PRODUCT_REQUIREMENTS.md | weight: 0.8 | hash: b94185cd

The new system replaces several existing manual and batch processes. Per-merchant spreadsheets for data management are being replaced by a centralized PostgreSQL database. The email-based order flow will be superseded by a REST API with automated state transitions, and nightly batch inventory reconciliation will be updated to a real-time synchronization process.

The migration strategy is phased, beginning with Phase 1 where the new API is deployed alongside existing tools, allowing merchants to opt in. Phase 2 involves importing two years of historical data per merchant, typically via CSV files. Finally, in Phase 3, old workflows will be sunsetted only after each merchant confirms full parity with the new system.

