# Constraints and Hard Rules

In scope for v1 are product catalog management, order lifecycle, customer accounts, and inventory tracking across warehouses. Out of scope for v1, and considered for v2, are marketplace/multi-vendor support, subscription billing, and analytics dashboards.

A critical success criterion is achieving zero inventory discrepancies after 30 days of parallel operation, emphasizing the requirement for high data accuracy and consistency.

> source: examples/sources/BUSINESS_DOMAIN_KNOWLEDGE.md | weight: 1.0 | hash: ae5c880e

Every API endpoint developed for the product requires JSON Web Token (JWT) authentication. This is a fundamental security requirement for all interactions with the system's APIs.

Order totals must be computed exclusively on the server-side. Any order totals supplied or calculated on the client-side will be ignored to prevent manipulation and ensure data integrity.

Inventory levels must never fall below zero. The system is designed to reject any order that would lead to an understocked product, preventing negative inventory counts.

Search functionality must return results within 200 milliseconds. This performance target applies to product catalogs containing up to 100,000 items, ensuring a fast search experience even for large inventories.

All processes involved in handling payments must comply with PCI-DSS standards. This ensures the secure processing, storage, and transmission of credit card data.

Sensitive user data must be encrypted both at rest and in transit. AES-256 encryption is mandated for data at rest, while TLS 1.3 is required for data in transit, ensuring robust data protection.

Public endpoints are subject to a rate limiting policy of 100 requests per minute per IP address. This measure helps to prevent abuse and ensure system stability.

Prices for products must be positive decimal numbers. They are required to be formatted with exactly two decimal places, ensuring consistency and accuracy in pricing.

Order status must adhere to a strict state machine progression. The defined states are pending, confirmed, shipped, and delivered, ensuring clear and consistent order tracking.

Hard deletes are not permitted for customers, orders, or products. Instead, all such entities must only be soft-deleted, allowing for potential recovery and maintaining historical data integrity.

If the order error rate exceeds 1% during the beta phase, the product rollout must be halted. This is a critical stop condition to ensure product quality before wider release.

Should the p95 latency on any endpoint exceed 5 seconds, a comprehensive performance review must be initiated. This threshold triggers an investigation into potential performance bottlenecks.

In the event of a critical failure, the system must be capable of reverting to the legacy workflow within 2 hours. This provides a rapid recovery mechanism for severe incidents.

> source: examples/sources/PRODUCT_REQUIREMENTS.md | weight: 0.8 | hash: b94185cd

A design decision was made to use PostgreSQL over DynamoDB, prioritizing data consistency, with the understanding that manual scaling will be acceptable. This choice reflects a trade-off favoring data integrity.

The initial API design favors REST over GraphQL, chosen for its simplicity for v1 clients. The team plans to revisit the decision regarding GraphQL at a later stage, implying a potential future evolution.

To prevent overselling, a synchronous inventory check is performed at checkout. This design decision introduces approximately 50ms of additional latency but is considered an acceptable trade-off for ensuring accurate stock levels.

The system has a target p95 latency of less than 200ms for displaying a product list, even with catalogs containing up to 100,000 products.

End-to-end order placement operations must complete within 3 seconds to ensure a smooth user experience.

Search functionality is targeted to respond in less than 200ms for a catalog of 100,000 items.

Inventory lookups are required to complete within 50ms per SKU, ensuring rapid stock availability checks.

