# Infrastructure and Integration

Currently, most merchants rely on a fragmented system landscape, using Shopify for their storefront, Google Sheets for inventory management, and email for order coordination. This results in data residing in multiple places, frequently leading to synchronization issues.

The platform aims for a customer-facing API uptime above 99.9% in the first quarter, setting a high bar for system reliability and performance.

> source: examples/sources/BUSINESS_DOMAIN_KNOWLEDGE.md | weight: 1.0 | hash: ae5c880e

The system will integrate with Shopify for inventory synchronization. This integration is planned to use webhooks, and the specific payload format for these webhooks needs to be defined.

External services like Stripe are integral for payment processing. The system must account for potential outages of such services, including implementing queuing and retry mechanisms for orders.

Product images and other assets will be stored on a service like S3. The system design includes considerations for S3 outages, where product pages would render without images but without a hard failure.

> source: examples/sources/PRODUCT_REQUIREMENTS.md | weight: 0.8 | hash: b94185cd

The platform's backend is built with Node.js/Express and uses PostgreSQL as its primary database. Redis is utilized for caching and rate limiting. Product images are stored in AWS S3, while payments are processed through Stripe, and transactional emails are sent via SendGrid. The frontend is a React Single Page Application (SPA) that consumes REST APIs.

Shopify will continue to serve as the storefront for merchants already using it; the new system will integrate with Shopify via webhooks. Stripe will remain the chosen payment processor for all transactions within the platform.

Key external dependencies include PostgreSQL version 15 or higher, which serves as the primary data store. Redis is used for managing user sessions, implementing rate limiting, and caching data. The Stripe API handles all payment processing, while AWS S3 stores product images. SendGrid is used for sending transactional emails.

The infrastructure is required to support 500 concurrent users while maintaining sub-second response times across the platform.

Database recovery capabilities must include point-in-time recovery, with a Recovery Point Objective (RPO) of no more than 5 minutes to minimize data loss.

Deployment processes will be Docker-based, enabling zero-downtime rolling updates to ensure continuous service availability.

